 \begin{abstract}
-Data gaps near the onset of black hole ringdown can obscure much of its rapidly decaying signal. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), a neural network that jointly denoises and reconstructs ringdown waveforms using complementary time--frequency and temporal representations. We evaluate DGF on 6,000 independent simulated LISA observations with gaps spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of each record. The mean noise-weighted mismatch, evaluated at fixed arrival time and phase, is $8.52\times10^{-3}$ across the test population and $5.32\times10^{-3}$ for network signal-to-noise ratios $\rho\ge10$. Mean absolute phase errors at the signal-envelope peak are below $4^\circ$ in both $A/E$ channels. These results demonstrate recovery of the oscillatory waveform across gaps, including intervals containing the signal peak, within the simulated source population and stationary Gaussian instrument-noise model.
+Data gaps near the onset of black hole ringdown can obscure much of its rapidly decaying signal. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), a neural network that jointly denoises and reconstructs ringdown waveforms using complementary time--frequency and temporal representations. We evaluate DGF on 6,000 independent simulated LISA observations with gaps spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of each record. For network signal-to-noise ratios $\rho\ge10$, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is $5.32\times10^{-3}$ at fixed arrival time and phase. The corresponding mean over all 6,000 test signals is $8.52\times10^{-3}$. Across the full test population, mean absolute phase errors at the signal-envelope peak are below $4^\circ$ in both $A/E$ channels. These results demonstrate recovery of the oscillatory waveform across gaps, including intervals containing the signal peak, within the simulated source population and stationary Gaussian instrument-noise model.
 \end{abstract}
 
